@page "/why-vibe-ui"

<PageTitle>Why Vibe.UI - Vibe.UI</PageTitle>

@*
    Every idea on this page carries a status: Available (on main) or Planned. Update the status,
    not the prose, when a planned piece lands.
*@

<div class="vibe-max-w-4xl vibe-mx-auto vibe-prose dark:vibe-prose-invert vibe-space-y-8">
    <header class="vibe-space-y-2">
        <h1 class="vibe-m-0">Why Vibe.UI</h1>
        <p class="vibe-m-0 vibe-text-muted-foreground">
            What the library is for, the ideas it is built on, where they come from, and what it
            deliberately leaves out.
        </p>
    </header>

    <p class="vibe-text-sm vibe-text-muted-foreground">
        Vibe.UI is pre-release. Each idea below is marked
        <Badge Variant="success">Available</Badge> when it is on the main branch today, or
        <Badge Variant="outline">Planned</Badge> when it is designed but not built.
    </p>

    <section>
        <h2>What it is for</h2>
        <p>
            Line-of-business applications built with Blazor. Most screens in those applications
            are one of three things, repeated many times: a list of records you page, sort and
            filter; a detail page for one record; and a form that creates or edits a record, over
            a real model with real validation rules.
        </p>
        <p>
            Vibe.UI aims to make those three screens short to write and consistent to use. It is
            not trying to cover every widget a component library could have.
        </p>
    </section>

    <section>
        <h2>The ideas</h2>

        <h3>Components are generic over your types</h3>
        <p>
            A value input binds the type of your model's property, such as an <code>int</code>,
            an enum, a <code>Guid</code> or a <code>DateOnly</code>, not a string you convert
            yourself. An enum needs no list of options; the select builds them.
        </p>
        <div class="vibe-rounded-xl vibe-border vibe-border-border vibe-bg-card vibe-p-6 vibe-space-y-3"
            data-testid="why-typed-select">
            <Select Id="why-order-status" Label="Order status" @bind-Value="orderStatus" />
            <p class="vibe-m-0 vibe-text-sm vibe-text-muted-foreground">
                Bound value: <code>@($"OrderStatus.{orderStatus}")</code>
            </p>
        </div>
        <CodeBlock Code="@TypedSelectCode" Language="razor" />
        <ul>
            <li><Badge Variant="success">Available</Badge> <code>Select</code>, <code>Combobox</code>, and <code>Input</code> for text, numbers, dates and times.</li>
            <li><Badge Variant="outline">Planned</Badge> <code>RadioGroup</code> and <code>ToggleGroup</code>.</li>
        </ul>

        <h3>Describe a property once</h3>
        <p>
            A field's label, its required marker and its length or range limits should come from
            the model, written down once, not repeated by hand on every form. Vibe.UI resolves
            them, in order, from a profile written in C#, a FluentValidation validator,
            DataAnnotations attributes, and finally the property name (<code>FirstName</code>
            becomes "First name"). The first one that answers wins, and a component can still
            override any of them where it is used.
        </p>
        <p>
            All validation goes through Blazor's own <code>EditContext</code>, so FluentValidation
            and DataAnnotations errors land in the same place and Vibe.UI keeps no error state of
            its own.
        </p>
        <ul>
            <li>
                <Badge Variant="success">Available</Badge> The metadata layer that resolves those
                sources, and a FluentValidation adapter whose <code>&lt;FluentValidator /&gt;</code>
                writes errors into the form's <code>EditContext</code>. The adapter is in the
                repository and is not on NuGet yet.
            </li>
            <li>
                <Badge Variant="outline">Planned</Badge> Components that read it: a form field that
                picks its control from the property's type and fills in its own label, required
                marker and error, so a form is one line per field.
            </li>
        </ul>

        <h3>Bind with expressions, not strings</h3>
        <p>
            Anything bound to a model takes an expression such as <code>x =&gt; x.Name</code>,
            so renaming a property is a compile error rather than a blank column.
        </p>
        <ul>
            <li>
                <Badge Variant="outline">Planned</Badge> as a rule for every data component. Today's
                <code>DataTable</code> still names its columns with strings and is due to be replaced.
            </li>
        </ul>

        <h3>Own the source, or reference the library</h3>
        <p>
            The <code>vibe</code> command line tool copies a component's source into your project,
            where you can read and change it like your own code. If you would rather take updates
            than own the code, reference the library instead.
        </p>
        <ul>
            <li>
                <Badge Variant="success">Available</Badge> The CLI and the library reference, both
                built from the repository.
            </li>
            <li><Badge Variant="outline">Planned</Badge> A beta package on NuGet.</li>
        </ul>
        <PreReleaseNotice />
    </section>

    <section>
        <h2>Where it comes from</h2>
        <p>Vibe.UI borrows from projects that did parts of this well. It is not affiliated with any of them.</p>
        <ul>
            <li>
                <a href="https://ui.shadcn.com/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">shadcn/ui</a>
                for the look and the ownership model: a CLI that copies component source into your
                project, theme colors as CSS variables, and Tailwind-style utility classes. Vibe.UI's
                theme token names follow shadcn/ui's.
            </li>
            <li>
                <a href="https://mudblazor.com/" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">MudBlazor</a>
                as the bar for completeness: what a Blazor team expects a component library to cover.
            </li>
            <li>
                Microsoft's
                <a href="https://learn.microsoft.com/aspnet/core/blazor/components/quickgrid" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">QuickGrid</a>
                as the grid to build on rather than replace. <Badge Variant="outline">Planned</Badge>
                Vibe.UI's data grid will wrap it with the theme, a paginator, rows that are real
                links, server paging through a provider, and column titles from the model.
            </li>
            <li>
                <a href="https://github.com/Narcoleptic-Fox/CRISP" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">CRISP</a>,
                Narcoleptic Fox's public application template, as the application Vibe.UI is designed
                against: data from an API as paged queries, validation in FluentValidation validators
                shared with the server. <Badge Variant="outline">Planned</Badge> A Blazor client for
                CRISP built on Vibe.UI.
            </li>
        </ul>
    </section>

    <section>
        <h2>What it refuses to do</h2>
        <p>
            These are rules for the redesign now under way. Where the library does not follow one
            yet, it says so.
        </p>
        <ul>
            <li>
                <strong>No second grid engine.</strong> Where QuickGrid can carry a table, Vibe.UI
                styles and extends it instead of writing its own.
            </li>
            <li>
                <strong>No race for Tailwind parity.</strong> The documented set of utility classes
                is the product. A utility is added when a real screen needs it.
            </li>
            <li>
                <strong>No component without a consumer.</strong> Nothing new is built until a real
                application needs it.
            </li>
            <li>
                <strong>No data API keyed by strings</strong>, not even as a "simple mode". Today's
                <code>DataTable</code> breaks this rule and is due to be replaced.
            </li>
            <li>
                <strong>No hierarchical rows, column drag-reorder or spreadsheet-style range
                selection.</strong> An application that needs those is better served by a
                commercial grid.
            </li>
            <li>
                <strong>No wrapper component where a utility class does the job.</strong>
            </li>
        </ul>
    </section>

    <section>
        <h2>Status</h2>
        <p>
            <strong>Available today:</strong> @DocsCatalog.Components.Count documented components,
            three themes in light and dark, the <code>vibe</code> CLI and the utility CSS, typed
            <code>Select</code>, <code>Combobox</code> and <code>Input</code>, the property metadata
            layer and the FluentValidation adapter. All
            of it is built from the repository; the beta is not on NuGet yet.
        </p>
        <p>
            <strong>Planned before the first beta:</strong> the remaining typed inputs, the
            one-line form field, the data grid on QuickGrid, links that know the current route,
            typed dialog results, and the NuGet beta.
        </p>
        <p>
            Progress happens in the open on
            <a href="https://github.com/Narcoleptic-Fox/Vibe.UI" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">GitHub</a>.
            To try what exists now, start with <a href="@GetUrl("installation")">Installation</a>.
        </p>
    </section>
</div>

@code {
    [Inject]
    private NavigationManager Navigation { get; set; } = default!;

    private enum OrderStatus
    {
        Draft,
        Submitted,
        Shipped,
        Cancelled
    }

    private OrderStatus orderStatus = OrderStatus.Submitted;

    private const string TypedSelectCode = """
        <Select Id="order-status" Label="Order status" @bind-Value="orderStatus" />

        @code {
            private enum OrderStatus { Draft, Submitted, Shipped, Cancelled }

            private OrderStatus orderStatus = OrderStatus.Submitted;
        }
        """;

    private string GetUrl(string path) => $"{Navigation.BaseUri}{path}";
}
